[gd_resource type="Resource" script_class="ManualPage" load_steps=2 format=3]

[ext_resource type="Script" path="res://src/components/manual_page.gd" id="1_page"]

[resource]
script = ExtResource("1_page")
title = "LIFE SUPPORT"
body = "CO2 is removed by the scrubber. Removal rate depends on power, on the filter, and on the blower's condition. A worn filter scrubs almost nothing while still drawing power.

FILTER SWAP - life-support side bay
  Take the wrench; check STORES for a CO2 filter.
  At the scrubber itself, press {interact} once for each prompt:
  1  POWER DOWN
  2  OPEN HOUSING (wrench in hand)
  3  REMOVE FILTER
  4  INSTALL FILTER (consumes one CO2 filter from stores)
  5  SEAT SEAL
  6  CLOSE HOUSING (wrench in hand)
  7  POWER UP
No loose filter needs carrying. This uses the unit's own switch, not the breaker menu. Restore the SCRUBBER breaker too if it was off; a powered-up unit on a shed circuit cannot scrub.

VERIFY. Check STATUS for the new filter condition, available scrubber power and the CO2 trend over time. A fresh filter does not repair a failed blower: diagnose at the ATMOSPHERE probe and use the DIAGNOSTICS refit procedure.

WATER. The reclaimer is in this same service bay, but its WATER test point is in the water closet. Low recovery can be a pump fault: multimeter at WATER, LIFE SUPPORT breaker OFF, wrench OPEN / REFIT WATER PUMP MAIN / CLOSE, then breaker ON. One gasket; see DIAGNOSTICS. Verify the pump fault clears and water recovery resumes.

WATCH THE FILTER CONDITION on STATUS. Below 50 percent, plan the swap. Below 20 percent, check the CO2 trend and do not defer a rising concentration."
